  pxB_p: "Where does an eliminated candidate's vote go? The 2022 runoff answers on real data: a transfer model fitted on 472,075 sections reproduces the held-out sections (R² = 0.976) and the national result to 0.7 pp. Retention is nearly perfect for the finalists (Lula 96.8%, Bolsonaro 99.8%) — the runoff is decided by the eliminated field.",
  pxB_n: "Read the anatomy of the center squeeze: Tebet — the eliminated center — split 2:1 to Bolsonaro, with 22% of her electorate spoiling the ballot or staying home; Ciro went 2:1 to Lula; Soraya's voters were 31% null. Every runoff number in Rule 3 comes from this measured matrix, not from polls.",
  px_transfers_cap: "Each row: where one round-1 vote type of 2022 went in the runoff, fitted on all sections simultaneously (numbers in %). Retention of the finalists is near-perfect — the fate of the runoff sits with the eliminated field.",
  px_transfers_alt: "Stacked bars of the 2022 round-1 to round-2 transfer matrix: finalists keep ~97–100%, eliminated candidates split",
  pxC_t: "Rule 3 · Only the top two — the predicted 25 October",
  pxC_p: "Apply the measured 2022 behavior to the 2026 votes: map each 2026 candidate to its 2022 origin by precinct profile (Bolsonaro→Bolsonaro ρ = 0.96, Lula→Lula 0.95, Cury/Ferreira/Caiado→Tebet), then run the transfer matrix. Predicted runoff: Bolsonaro 52.6%, Lula 47.4% — the fragmented round-1 majority re-assembles behind one name.",
  pxC_n: "Margins make the textbook paradoxes unreachable: displacing Bolsonaro from the top two would have taken a 52.7M-vote swing (his lead over third place), displacing Lula — 2.2M. No minor candidate is a decisive spoiler: removing the strongest of them (Cury, 3.4M votes) moves Lula's head-to-head share only from 47.4% to 48.7%. And the 2022 data shows the no-show side of runoffs: 22–41% of an eliminated candidate's voters spoil the ballot or stay home.",
  px_runoff_cap: "Left: the round-1 votes counted as two blocs — the anti-Lula bloc clears 50% outright. Right: the head-to-head predicted by the 2022 transfer model (95% CI; the 2022 backtest shows a +0.7 pp bias toward Lula).",
